    "explanation": "To the rider and the driver both. This case cannot be decided on the present record, so it goes to a human reviewer. The disputed $34.42 is held — neither collected nor refunded — until that review is complete. No money moves now.\n\n1. The one measurement this case turns on is broken exactly where it matters. The GPS trace (EV-GPS-00110) has a single 428-second gap, between 18:35:00 and 18:42:08, inside the KPE tunnel — the very stretch the rider disputes. 428 seconds is far past the 90-second limb in POL-RD-07, so the trace is impaired for policy purposes, not merely flagged by the admissibility pre-pass. It carries weight 0.39, with issue codes GPS_GAP_LONG_INSIDE_WINDOW and LOW_COVERAGE (coverage 81.7%). Under POL-GEN-02(3) the finding is stated to survive that discount only as a finding of impairment, and it is the impairment itself that drives this ruling.\n\n2. The trace cannot answer the question either way. Read the way POL-RD-07(i) directs — a straight line across the gap, the reading most favourable to the driver — the trip measures 21.20 km against the booked optimal of 18.40 km: an excess of 2.80 km, or 15.2%, below POL-RD-01's 20% limb. But a surface route between the same two portals would measure 25.50 km and clear both limbs. Nothing in the bundle distinguishes the two readings, and the gap covers the entire disputed segment. The governing distance is therefore genuinely unknown.\n\n3. No independent corroboration exists. POL-RD-07(ii) forbids establishing a deviation on an impaired trace alone and names the sources that can stand in. I have checked each. The chat log (EV-CHAT-00110, weight 0.60, issue TRUNCATED_LOG) contains no admission of a detour — only the driver's 'going KPE ah boss' and the rider's 'ok', and the KPE is the optimal corridor itself. The fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00110, weight 0.75, issue PARTIAL_RECORD) has erp_gantries null, so no charged gantry places the car on a corridor the optimal route does not use. The driver's statement claims the KPE, which is the route the platform itself planned. And the billed 21.20 km is a copy of the trace figure, which POL-RD-07(ii) says is not independent corroboration.\n\n4. POL-RD-07(iii) and POL-RD-10(3) therefore require ESCALATE rather than NO_ACTION: the claim is unproved on this record, but a 428-second hole over the whole disputed stretch is a data failure, not a finding for the driver. Escalation is terminal, so no monetary outcome issues and nothing is executed.\n\nClaims addressed. Driver: your POL-RD-01 arithmetic is correct (2.80 km, 15.2%, below the 20% gate) and your POL-RD-07(i) point — that the mandated reading gives the driver-favourable figure and does not enlarge the excess — is correct too; but on an impaired trace that arithmetic cannot be the last word, and POL-RD-07(iii) sends the case to a human instead of NO_ACTION. Driver: no traffic advisory is on file (EV-ADVIS-00110-01 is MISSING, weight 0.0), so no closure-adjusted optimal exists and the booked 18.40 km stands (POL-RD-03, POL-RD-13). Driver: no repeat-deviation pattern is shown — driver_profile.deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 744 completed trips in 90 days — so no POL-RD-06(c) conduct referral fires. Driver: the $3.00 ERP is not a billing-integrity defect — no gantry is itemised (the match is undecidable and treated as matched), and the optimal route itself crosses both gantries — so no POL-RD-09(4) referral fires. Rider: your POL-RD-02 arithmetic (a refund in the region of $5.74 to $8.74, above the $0.50 de minimis and short of the 90% full-refund share) is not reached; POL-RD-02 runs only once a deviation is established, and none is established here. Rider: your time-based narrative — 39 minutes, and support not producing a route map — is not distance evidence under POL-RD-01.\n\nWhat would resolve it: an independent measure of the distance actually driven across the KPE gap — a gantry-by-gantry ERP record placing the car on or off the optimal corridor, a second GPS source, or a statement from either party admitting the route taken. That is the material the reviewer needs."
